>> title: Corned Beef, Instant Pot
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: beef, cured, brisket, deli, instant pot, pressure cooker
>> counters: Instant Pot
>> dish: corned-beef
>> kit: Instant Pot
>> aka: corned beef brisket, salt beef, boiled beef, corn beef, brined brisket, instant pot corned beef, pressure cooker corned beef
>> pairs-with: pastrami, sauerkraut, russian-dressing, deli-rye-bread
>> servings: 6
>> time: 5 days 4 hr 30 min
>> slack: forgiving — a five-day cure is five days give or take half of one, and ninety minutes at pressure cannot overcook a brisket into anything worse than softer; the two-hour desalting soak is the step with no give, because a brisket that skips it is too salty to eat and nothing later dilutes it
>> step.2: stir the brine
>> step.3: brine 5 days, turn daily
>> step.4: rinse, soak 2 hr
>> step.5: 90 min high pressure, natural release 20 min
>> step.6: rest 20 min, slice across the grain

Only the simmer moves. The five-day cure and the two-hour desalting soak are the same as the plain way, ingredient for ingredient, and no part of them is pressure's to give back. Three pounds rather than five, because a 6-quart pot will not take a five-pound flat under its fill line.

Stir @water{4%L}(1 gal, cold), @kosher salt{200%g}(1 cup Diamond Crystal), @dark brown sugar{100%g}(1/2 cup packed), @pink curing salt{25%g}(5 tsp; cure #1), @pickling spice{4%Tbs} and @garlic{10%cloves}(crushed) until the salt and sugar have gone.

Submerge @beef brisket{3%lb}(1.4 kg, flat cut, fat cap left on) in @&(~1)brine{} in a #non-reactive tub{}, weight it under the surface, and ~brine{5%days} in the fridge, turning it over once a day.

Rinse @&(~1)cured brisket{} and ~soak{2%hr} in @cold water{4%L}(1 gal, changed once). Fry a coin to taste: salt can still be taken out here and not once the lid is on.

Pressure cook @&(~1)soaked brisket{} with @water{6%cup}(1.4 L; just to cover it), @yellow onion{1%large}(halved), @carrots{2}, @celery{2%ribs}, @bay leaves{2}, @black peppercorns{1%tsp}, lid on and valve sealed: ~come to pressure{20%min}, then ~pressure cook{90%min} on high and ~natural release{20%min}. A quick release here boils the brisket from the inside and squeezes it into string.

Lift @&(~1)brisket{} out of its liquid, ~rest{20%min} under foil, and carve it across the grain, which turns ninety degrees partway along the flat.
